---
import Shell from '../../layouts/Shell.astro';
import atlas from '../../../../../kb/research/voices/population-2011.json';
import {multilingualTotals,count,percent} from '../../lib/language-atlas.mjs';
import '../../styles/language-districts.css';
const t=multilingualTotals(atlas);
const segments=[{label:'No additional language reported',value:t.noAdditionalReported},{label:'One additional language reported',value:t.exactlyTwoReported},{label:'Two additional languages reported',value:t.three}];
---
<Shell title="Multilingual Odisha · beyond one mother tongue" description="Understand Odisha’s Census 2011 multilingualism: mother tongue, additional languages and why language does not determine ancestry." active="explore" wide related={false}>
<article class="district-wrap"><header class="district-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Languages travel together · Census 2011</p><h1>One person.<br/><em>More than one way to speak.</em></h1><p>A home language, a workplace conversation, a favourite song: one name in a Census table cannot describe every part of a person’s linguistic life.</p></header>
<div class="district-stats"><div><span>At least one additional language reported</span><strong>{count(t.atLeastTwo)}</strong><span>{percent(t.atLeastTwo,t.population)} of Odisha’s 2011 population</span></div><div><span>Within that total: two additional languages</span><strong>{count(t.three)}</strong><span>{percent(t.three,t.population)} of the same population</span></div></div>
<section class="district-section"><h2>Three distinct slices of the same population</h2><p>The bars below do not overlap. The first headline above combines the second and third bars; adding that headline to the third bar would count some people twice.</p>{segments.map(s=><div class="district-copy"><p><strong>{s.label}</strong> · {count(s.value)} · {percent(s.value,t.population)}</p><span class="district-bar" aria-hidden="true"><i style={'width:'+s.value/t.population*100+'%'}></i></span></div>)}<p class="district-small">Denominator: {count(t.population)} people, Odisha, Census 2011. C-17 records up to two additional languages. “None reported” is not proof that a person was unable to use another language.</p></section>
<section class="district-section district-copy"><h2>A mother tongue is one question</h2><p>C-16 records mother-tongue returns. C-17 records additional languages against Census language groups. The latter describes the state as a whole in this dataset; it cannot tell us a district’s multilingual rate or the skills of an individual.</p><p>A speaker’s language does not by itself establish birthplace, migration history or ancestry. Those questions need their own evidence and, where appropriate, a person’s own account.</p></section>
<section class="district-section district-copy"><h2>Listen before choosing the label</h2><p>Ask someone which language they prefer, how they name it and whether they would like to share a word, a reading recommendation or a song. Keep their name for the language alongside the source’s terminology when the two differ.</p><p>On a visit, confirm communication needs with your host. A district’s Census majority is useful historical background; it is not a promise about a guide, a household or a village.</p></section>
<nav class="district-links" aria-label="Continue exploring"><a href="/languages/districts/">Explore district profiles →</a><a href="/languages/atlas/">Compare language groups →</a><a href="/languages/">Meet the language stories →</a></nav>
<details class="mag-article-credits"><summary>Sources & calculation</summary><p><a href={atlas.sources.c17.url}>Census C-17, Odisha, 2011</a>, checked against <a href={atlas.sources.c16.url}>C-16</a>. State totals sum the 90 mutually exclusive language groups, including OTHERS. People reporting two additional languages are a subset of those reporting at least one. One-additional-only is their difference; none-reported is the population minus the at-least-one count.</p><p>Figures describe 2011 returns, not present-day fluency or a language-vitality ranking. Source workbooks, row references and calculations are preserved in the project KB.</p></details></article>
</Shell>
